WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(sound waves vibrating)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.370
- [Narrator] Here's a sound wave.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.650
And here's its inverse.
(sound wave beeping)

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.674
Combine them and you have silence.

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.210
(upbeat music)
That's the basic idea

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.520
behind noise-canceling headphones.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.410
But it takes a lot of technology

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.650
to make that seemingly
simple science possible.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:20.040
- We have 15 millionths of a second

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.430
to measure the noise, run it
through a little DSP processor,

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.190
compute that inverse signal,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.320
and pop it out through the driver.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.562
- [Narrator] Here's how they work.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:32.130
(digital signaling)

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.170
- [John] One way to
visualize acoustic waves,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.060
that I like to use, is a Slinky.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.400
- [Narrator] John Rule
has spent the last decade

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.020
developing new algorithms

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.420
for personalizing noise
cancellation performance,

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.830
in Bose Headphones.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.150
- [John] We draw them as sine waves,

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.970
as if something's going back
and forth or up and down.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.260
But what's really happening,

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.660
is that these vibrations
are actually propagating

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:52.860
through the air.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.660
The medium has stayed fixed,

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.670
but you can see that pulse of energy

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.590
sort of running through the length of it.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.750
- [Narrator] Because sound is just waves,

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:02.160
you can cancel a sound wave

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.250
by introducing an equal and opposite wave.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:09.000
- So what you've got is you've
got an incoming sound wave.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.860
And the way destructive interference works

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.900
is we say, "Hey, if I could generate

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.330
an equal and opposite signal, right?

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.923
I could generate the signal,

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.340
that's the inverse of that.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.620
And if I sum those two things together,

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.120
I'll be left with a flat line.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.980
I'll be left with zero pressure,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.350
and that will be interpreted by my brain

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.060
as the noise having been canceled."

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.680
- [Narrator] Bose was the first company

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.680
to develop Noise Reduction
Technology for headphones.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.470
- Dr. Bose was coming
back on an overseas flight

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.390
and got very frustrated by the fact,

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.130
that airplanes were very noisy.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.470
We can control all sorts of other stuff,

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.910
why can't we control noise?

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.560
The first application that we looked

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.830
at was of canceling noise on airplanes.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:53.640
- [Narrator] Pilots wore its
earliest prototypes in 1986.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.460
While the technology has
gotten more sophisticated,

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.710
the basic science remained the same.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:02.370
Active Noise Cancellation
requires a few main components,

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.710
an outer microphone to
track incoming noise,

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.720
an inner microphone
that tracks sound waves

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.130
that make it inside your ear,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:11.160
and also checks if more
environmental noise than expected,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.330
is making it through,

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.060
a digital signal processor
to run some algorithms,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.850
based on data from the
mics, and the speaker,

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.350
which cancels the background noise,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.650
while also playing the music
or sound you want to hear.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.350
- Think about how fast this has to happen.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:26.910
The sound is obviously propagating

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.380
at the speed of sound, right?

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.360
Which is 343 meters per second.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.740
If you think about that in terms

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.630
of the thickness of an earbud,

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.790
it's getting from the
outside of the earbud

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.720
to the inside of the earbud
in about 15 microseconds,

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.520
so that's millionths of a second.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.010
We're doing math as fast as we can

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.500
to basically, cancel it
out before it gets in.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.140
- [Narrator] Because everyone's
ears are differently shaped,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.660
Active Noise Cancellation
requires algorithms

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.000
that can be tuned, based on the user.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.754
Bose, for instance, does this
by playing a welcome chime,

00:02:56.754 --> 00:02:58.590
(earphones chiming)
and monitoring feedback

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.030
from the internal microphone

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.760
to customize noise-canceling
filters, accordingly.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.710
This requires a lot of testing,

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.830
to make sure the algorithms
work on a wide range of users,

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.990
in all types of noisy environments.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:13.230
But eliminating background
noise is just the first step.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.080
The next, is figuring
out what noise to remove,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.430
based on the context.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:18.990
- I think it's really
important to recognize

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.520
that noise is not bad.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.580
Noise is critical to my
brain, critical to my body.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.430
My brain uses it to create
richer experiences to fill

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:29.070
in what's missing, which is all the time.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.540
- [Narrator] Poppy Crum was previously

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.060
the Chief Scientist at Dolby Laboratories,

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.220
where she worked for more than a decade.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.100
She's led teams focused
on Emergent Technologies

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.800
and platforms that impact sensory systems.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.590
- When you think about
Noise-Canceling Technology,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.760
I like to sort of separate it

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.050
into three different categories.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.480
You know, there's Passive Noise-Canceling,

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.360
or Passive Noise-Reduction,
which is effectively an earplug.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.970
The second layer would be
Active Noise-Reduction.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:55.710
I mean, it's dynamic to the system,

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.210
but it's dealing with what we would call,

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:01.050
stationary sound, sounds that
are, you know, consistent,

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.210
they're continuous or they're predictable.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.010
Examples of that might be

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:08.010
a generator or airplane noise.
(generator roaring)

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.840
You know, not all the
sounds in the airplane,

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.600
but that general rumble,
those low frequency sounds

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.030
you're trying to get rid of,
they're pretty predictable.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.920
- [Narrator] For these predictable noises,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.630
current noise-canceling algorithms

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:19.980
can quickly identify
(waves crashing)

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.090
unwanted sound waves

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.560
and cancel them out.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.930
Beyond that, things get harder.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:25.890
- And then the next layer,
(fan rumbling)

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:26.970
which is I think where, you know,

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.290
the future's still evolving,

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.110
is when you start being able
to really cancel out things

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:33.510
that are what you would
call non-stationary noise.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:34.440
Things that might be

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.340
a dog barking.
(dog barking)

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.560
And you know, those elements that happen,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:38.700
where you can't predict 'em
(crowd murmuring)

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.790
and you might be able
to have a expectation

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.410
that they're going to happen,

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.840
but they're things that aren't continuous

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.010
and aren't constant.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.900
- [Narrator] But sometimes,
after your technology

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.080
has gone through so many steps
to reduce background noise,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.430
you want it back.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.770
AirPods Pro have a Transparency Mode,

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.590
where they recreate the normal
sound of your environment.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.290
Other noise canceling
earbuds also offer modes

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.060
to help you hear your surroundings,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:06.360
like Bose's Aware Mode and
Sony's Ambient Sound Mode,

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.220
that preserve enough background noise

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.050
to keep you alert to your surroundings.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.660
- When you build a technology
that's supposed to map

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:13.830
to a human experience,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.930
you need to understand
how the human system

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.530
perceives change or perceives
that element you're building,

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.990
and then that becomes how
your algorithm behaves,

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:24.180
so that you have a continuous
experience of the world.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.400
So you need labs where you can remove

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.249
or control every other element.

00:05:28.249 --> 00:05:31.020
- [Narrator] Noise-cancellation
could soon solve

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.960
more complex problems,
like using machine learning

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:35.970
to seamlessly and intelligently

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:40.500
eliminate unwanted sounds or
voices across various contexts.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.240
Adaptive Audio, which is coming

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.580
to Apple's AirPods Pro soon,

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.910
combines noise-cancellation
with the Transparency Mode,

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.564
so you can only hear the
important sounds around you.

00:05:50.564 --> 00:05:51.397
(bike bell ringing)

00:05:51.397 --> 00:05:52.726
- [Pedestrian] So you can stay present

00:05:52.726 --> 00:05:54.167
within your environment,

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:55.812
while distracting noises
are automatically reduced.

00:05:55.812 --> 00:05:57.540
(leaf blower blowing)

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.180
- It's the thing that
everybody wants, right?

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.370
They wanna make certain noises go away.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:03.750
So they still wanna hear some things,

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:04.950
they don't wanna hear other things.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:06.930
And "Can you just gimme
a set of headphones

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:08.160
that, you know, just cancels the stuff

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.450
I don't want to hear."

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.700
That's not a thing that
we're offering yet,

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.380
but that's the kind of thing

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:14.730
that is being worked on.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.897
(upbeat music ending)

